---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import PipelineDiagram from '@/components/PipelineDiagram.astro'

const exporterEnv = `# The base URL. Your exporter appends /v1/traces itself.
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/json

# Optional. Send gzip if your payloads are large.
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_COMPRESSION=gzip

# Only when mcpd runs with --ingest-key.
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS="Authorization=Bearer YOUR_INGEST_KEY"`

const rawPost = `curl -X POST http://localhost:4317/v1/traces \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{
  "resourceSpans": [{
    "resource": { "attributes": [
      { "key": "service.name", "value": { "stringValue": "checkout-agent" } }
    ]},
    "scopeSpans": [{
      "spans": [{
        "traceId": "4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736",
        "spanId": "00f067aa0ba902b7",
        "name": "chat gpt-4o",
        "startTimeUnixNano": "1700000000000000000",
        "endTimeUnixNano": "1700000001500000000",
        "status": { "code": 1 },
        "attributes": [
          { "key": "gen_ai.system", "value": { "stringValue": "openai" } },
          { "key": "gen_ai.request.model", "value": { "stringValue": "gpt-4o" } },
          { "key": "gen_ai.operation.name", "value": { "stringValue": "chat" } },
          { "key": "gen_ai.usage.input_tokens", "value": { "intValue": "1200" } },
          { "key": "gen_ai.usage.output_tokens", "value": { "intValue": "350" } }
        ]
      }]
    }]
  }]
}'

# {"partialSuccess":{"rejectedSpans":0,"errorMessage":""}}`
---

<Doc
  title="OpenTelemetry"
  description="mcpd accepts OTLP/JSON on /v1/traces, /v1/logs and /v1/metrics, and reads both the OpenTelemetry gen_ai.* and the OpenInference llm.* semantic conventions. No mcpd-specific code required."
  lede="If your agent already emits OpenTelemetry, mcpd is an endpoint change. This page is the whole OTLP path: the three endpoints, the encoding, and every attribute mcpd reads."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>The three endpoints</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd serves the OTLP HTTP endpoints on the same port as its query API and
    its interface. There is no collector to run in front of it, no second
    container, and no separate receiver port.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Endpoint</th>
          <th>Accepts</th>
          <th>Answers with</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /v1/traces</code></td>
          <td><code>ExportTraceServiceRequest</code></td>
          <td><code>partialSuccess.rejectedSpans</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /v1/logs</code></td>
          <td><code>ExportLogsServiceRequest</code></td>
          <td><code>partialSuccess.rejectedLogRecords</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /v1/metrics</code></td>
          <td><code>ExportMetricsServiceRequest</code></td>
          <td><code>partialSuccess.rejectedDataPoints</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Every successful export answers with OTLP's own <code>partialSuccess</code>
    shape carrying a count. That is how a collector tells a sender it dropped
    something, and exporters read it. Returning an empty body would let a sender
    report a success it did not get.
  </p>

  <h2>Point an exporter at it</h2>
  <p>
    Two variables do the work. Set them in the environment of the process that
    runs your agent, and nothing else changes.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="exporter"
    samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'Environment', shiki: 'bash', code: exporterEnv }]}
  />

  <p>
    Two details are worth knowing before you debug a silence. First,
    <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> is a base URL and the exporter
    appends the signal path to it. Set
    <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT</code> instead if you want to give
    the full URL, path included. Second, port 4317 is conventionally the gRPC
    port in OpenTelemetry and 4318 the HTTP one. mcpd serves HTTP on 4317
    because it serves everything on one port, so an exporter left on its default
    port sends to 4318 and you see nothing arrive. Name the port explicitly.
  </p>

  <h2>Both encodings, for traces</h2>
  <p>
    <code>/v1/traces</code> reads the <code>content-type</code> header and
    accepts either encoding: OTLP/protobuf when it contains
    <code>protobuf</code>, OTLP/JSON otherwise. Protobuf is the OpenTelemetry
    default, so an unmodified SDK pointed at mcpd sends traces that land without
    any configuration at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>/v1/logs</code> and <code>/v1/metrics</code> accept JSON only. A
    protobuf body there is refused as <code>body is not valid JSON</code>. If
    you are sending all three signals, set the protocol once, globally:
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="json for every signal"
    command="export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/json"
  />

  <p>
    When no <code>content-type</code> is sent at all, mcpd assumes
    <code>application/json</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Compression and size</h2>
  <p>
    A request body may arrive with <code>content-encoding: gzip</code>, with
    <code>identity</code>, or with no encoding header. Anything else is refused
    by name: <code>content-encoding "br" is not supported; use gzip or send the
    body uncompressed</code>. Responses are compressed by content negotiation,
    so a large trace read back over a slow link is compressed too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two limits apply to one request. The body limit is 32 MiB and is measured
    on what arrives, so gzip buys you room. The batch limit is 10,000 signals,
    counted after decoding on every ingest door: spans on
    <code>/v1/traces</code>, records on <code>/v1/logs</code>, data points on
    <code>/v1/metrics</code> and everything in the request on
    <code>/v1/ingest</code>. A larger batch is refused with
    <code>413 payload_too_large</code> naming both numbers. Both limits are
    settings: <code>--max-body-bytes</code> and
    <code>--max-batch-signals</code>, described on
    <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">Configuration</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens between arriving and being stored</h2>
  <p>
    An OTLP span and a native span take the same path. That is the point of the
    pipeline being shared: the order below cannot drift between the two doors.
  </p>

  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    Costing at ingest is why an OTLP span gets a cost figure at all. mcpd prices
    it from the price book this server is running with, at the moment it
    arrives, so changing your rates later cannot silently rewrite what last
    month cost. See <a href="/docs/cost" class="link">Cost accounting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Both semantic conventions</h2>
  <p>
    Two competing sets of attribute names are in use for AI spans:
    OpenTelemetry's own <code>gen_ai.*</code> and OpenInference's
    <code>llm.*</code> with <code>openinference.span.kind</code>. mcpd reads
    both, and its own <code>mcpd.*</code> names on top. You do not rename
    anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each row below is read in order, first match wins. An attribute that no row
    names is still kept: the whole flattened attribute map is stored on the
    span, so nothing you send is lost by not being mapped.
  </p>

  <h3>Model, payload and usage</h3>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>mcpd field</th>
          <th>Read from, in order</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>model</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.request.model</code>, <code>gen_ai.response.model</code>, <code>llm.model_name</code>, <code>mcpd.model</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>provider</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.system</code>, <code>llm.provider</code>, <code>mcpd.provider</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>input</code></td>
          <td><code>input.value</code>, <code>gen_ai.prompt</code>, <code>mcpd.input</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>output</code></td>
          <td><code>output.value</code>, <code>gen_ai.completion</code>, <code>mcpd.output</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>entity_name</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.agent.name</code>, <code>mcpd.entity.name</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>usage.input_tokens</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.usage.input_tokens</code>, <code>gen_ai.usage.prompt_tokens</code>, <code>llm.token_count.prompt</code>, <code>mcpd.usage.input_tokens</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>usage.output_tokens</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.usage.output_tokens</code>, <code>gen_ai.usage.completion_tokens</code>, <code>llm.token_count.completion</code>, <code>mcpd.usage.output_tokens</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>usage.total_tokens</code></td>
          <td><code>llm.token_count.total</code>, <code>gen_ai.usage.total_tokens</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>input_details.cache_read</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.usage.cache_read_input_tokens</code>, <code>llm.token_count.prompt_details.cache_read</code>, <code>mcpd.usage.cache_read_tokens</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>input_details.cache_write</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.usage.cache_creation_input_tokens</code>, <code>llm.token_count.prompt_details.cache_write</code>, <code>mcpd.usage.cache_write_tokens</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>input_details.audio</code></td>
          <td><code>llm.token_count.prompt_details.audio</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>output_details.reasoning</code></td>
          <td><code>llm.token_count.completion_details.reasoning</code>, <code>gen_ai.usage.reasoning_tokens</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>output_details.audio</code></td>
          <td><code>llm.token_count.completion_details.audio</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    A span with none of the three token counts carries no usage object at all,
    rather than one full of zeroes. Zero input tokens and unknown input tokens
    are different facts, and a cost report built on the first when it meant the
    second is wrong in a way nobody notices.
  </p>

  <h3>Correlation</h3>
  <p>
    Correlation fields are what let you ask for one user's runs, or one
    session's. Span attributes are read first, then resource attributes.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>mcpd field</th>
          <th>Read from, in order</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>project</code></td>
          <td><code>mcpd.project</code> on the span, then on the resource, then the daemon's <code>--project</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>environment</code></td>
          <td><code>mcpd.environment</code>, resource <code>deployment.environment.name</code>, resource <code>deployment.environment</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>service_name</code></td>
          <td>resource <code>service.name</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>service_version</code></td>
          <td>resource <code>service.version</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>session_id</code></td>
          <td><code>gen_ai.conversation.id</code>, <code>session.id</code>, <code>mcpd.session_id</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>thread_id</code></td>
          <td><code>mcpd.thread_id</code>, <code>thread.id</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>user_id</code></td>
          <td><code>user.id</code>, <code>enduser.id</code>, <code>mcpd.user_id</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>request_id</code></td>
          <td><code>mcpd.request_id</code>, <code>http.request.id</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>release</code></td>
          <td><code>mcpd.release</code>, resource <code>service.version</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>run_id</code>, <code>resource_id</code>, <code>experiment_id</code></td>
          <td><code>mcpd.run_id</code>, <code>mcpd.resource_id</code>, <code>mcpd.experiment_id</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>tags</code></td>
          <td><code>mcpd.tags</code>, as an array of strings</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Status and errors</h3>
  <p>
    OTLP status code <code>1</code> becomes <code>ok</code> and <code>2</code>
    becomes <code>error</code>. An unset status with an end time is recorded as
    <code>ok</code>, because a span that finished without complaint did.
    An unset status with no end time stays <code>unset</code>: the span is still
    running, and mcpd stores it that way rather than inventing an outcome.
  </p>
  <p>
    On an error span, the message comes from <code>status.message</code>, then
    <code>exception.message</code>, and failing both the literal string
    <code>unspecified error</code>. The error kind comes from
    <code>exception.type</code> and the stack from
    <code>exception.stacktrace</code>. An error span always carries a message,
    because "something failed" with no text is not something you can act on.
  </p>

  <h3>Span kind</h3>
  <p>
    The kind drives how a span is drawn in the waterfall. mcpd infers it in
    order of specificity, and falls back to <code>generic</code>, which is
    always safe. Mislabelling a span is worse than declining to label it.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><code>mcpd.span.kind</code>, when it names a kind mcpd knows.</li>
    <li><code>openinference.span.kind</code>.</li>
    <li><code>gen_ai.operation.name</code>.</li>
    <li>The presence of an attribute that only one kind of span carries.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Attribute value</th>
          <th>mcpd kind</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=LLM</code></td><td><code>model_generation</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=CHAIN</code></td><td><code>workflow_run</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=TOOL</code></td><td><code>tool_call</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=AGENT</code></td><td><code>agent_run</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=RETRIEVER</code></td><td><code>retrieval</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=EMBEDDING</code></td><td><code>rag_embedding</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=RERANKER</code></td><td><code>rag_action</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=GUARDRAIL</code></td><td><code>guardrail</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>openinference.span.kind=EVALUATOR</code></td><td><code>scorer_run</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gen_ai.operation.name=chat</code></td><td><code>model_generation</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gen_ai.operation.name=text_completion</code></td><td><code>model_generation</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gen_ai.operation.name=generate_content</code></td><td><code>model_generation</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gen_ai.operation.name=embeddings</code></td><td><code>rag_embedding</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gen_ai.operation.name=execute_tool</code></td><td><code>tool_call</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gen_ai.operation.name=invoke_agent</code></td><td><code>agent_run</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gen_ai.operation.name=create_agent</code></td><td><code>agent_run</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>any of <code>gen_ai.system</code>, <code>llm.model_name</code>, <code>llm.provider</code> present</td><td><code>model_generation</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mcp.tool.name</code> present</td><td><code>mcp_tool_call</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>http.request.method</code> or <code>http.method</code> present</td><td><code>http_request</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>anything else</td><td><code>generic</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    A span kind mcpd does not recognise under
    <code>openinference.span.kind</code> or
    <code>gen_ai.operation.name</code> falls to <code>generic</code> rather
    than being rejected. The full list of kinds is in the
    <a href="/docs/reference/data-model" class="link">data model</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Logs and metrics</h2>
  <p>
    Log records map by severity number, the way the specification prescribes:
    1 to 4 is trace, 5 to 8 debug, 9 to 12 info, 13 to 16 warn, 17 to 20 error,
    and anything above that is fatal. With no severity number, a record is
    treated as info. The message comes from <code>body.stringValue</code>, the
    logger name from the scope name, and <code>traceId</code> and
    <code>spanId</code> are kept when they are valid, which is what puts a log
    line next to the span that emitted it.
  </p>
  <p>
    For metrics, sums become counters and gauges become gauges. Histograms are
    recorded by their sum and count with the bucket boundaries preserved: mcpd
    does not pretend it can re-derive an exact quantile from a pre-aggregated
    histogram. Exemplars are kept, which is what lets a spike on a chart open
    the run that caused it. Most tools discard them.
  </p>

  <h2>Ids, and W3C traceparent</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd uses OpenTelemetry's identifier shapes exactly: a trace id is 128 bits
    as 32 lowercase hex characters, a span id is 64 bits as 16. Keeping the wire
    format identical is what removes the translation layer. The all-zero id is
    reserved to mean "no trace" and is refused wherever it appears, including in
    ids mcpd generates itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    OTLP/JSON specifies hex for id fields, but several SDKs emit base64. mcpd
    accepts both and stores hex. A span whose ids are missing, the wrong length
    or all-zero is skipped and the rest of the batch is still written, because a
    collector that rejects ten thousand good spans over one bad one is a
    collector people turn off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Timestamps are nanoseconds since the Unix epoch. They exceed 2<sup>53</sup>,
    so OTLP/JSON sends them as strings, and mcpd parses them as strings. Reading
    them as floating point loses precision, which shows up as spans that appear
    to start microseconds apart when they did not. An
    <code>endTimeUnixNano</code> of <code>0</code> or absent means the span has
    not finished.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <code>traceparent</code> header is
    <code>version-traceid-parentid-flags</code>. mcpd parses it with the rules
    the specification sets: version <code>ff</code> is refused, version
    <code>00</code> must have exactly four fields, later versions may carry
    more, the ids must validate, and bit 0 of the flags byte is the sampled
    flag. A malformed header is never a reason to refuse a request. mcpd starts
    a new trace instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where this matters: the mcpd SDKs read an inbound <code>traceparent</code>
    and emit one on outbound calls, so a trace survives a service boundary. The
    OTLP endpoints take their ids from the payload, because your exporter has
    already put them in the spans. If a trace begins in a service you do not
    instrument with mcpd, pass its <code>traceparent</code> along and both
    halves land under one trace id.
  </p>

  <h2>One request, in full</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing above needs an SDK. This is a complete, valid export you can paste
    into a terminal:
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="raw OTLP/JSON"
    samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'Shell', shiki: 'bash', code: rawPost }]}
  />

  <h2>What OTLP cannot carry</h2>
  <p>
    OTLP has a shape for spans, logs and metrics. It has none for a machine
    score, a human's thumbs-down or an aspect note, and no vocabulary for the
    token detail a modern provider bills on. Two ways to send those:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>POST /v1/ingest</code>, mcpd's native batch endpoint, which carries
      the full data model in one request.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>POST /v1/api/scores</code> and <code>POST /v1/api/feedback</code>,
      which attach to a trace you have already sent over OTLP.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Mixing the two is normal and expected. Export spans with the OpenTelemetry
    SDK you already run, and post scores from your evaluation job. They join on
    the trace id. See
    <a href="/docs/evaluation" class="link">Scores and feedback</a> and the
    <a href="/docs/reference/api" class="link">HTTP API</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you expose the port</h2>
  <p>
    With no <code>--ingest-key</code> set, anyone who can reach 4317 can write
    to it, and with no <code>--read-key</code>, read every stored prompt. mcpd
    logs a warning at startup when it binds a non-loopback address with either
    left open. Set them with
    <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">configuration</a>, then send the
    key as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>, which is what
    <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code> above does.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    label="run with keys"
    command="mcpd --ingest-key $INGEST_KEY --read-key $READ_KEY"
  />
</Doc>
